From caede65f423fb6d1326e70315d9da5b58e41383e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aleksei Bukhalov <buhaloff.aleks@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 13:50:26 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 17/17] feat: shrink the disk tier's fixed cost (Phase M2 lever
 3): write-temp-then-rename, optional quantized KV entries

Two independent changes to the per-entry fixed cost (whole-state file,
read, deserialize) that the cost-model admission check exists to weigh
against a recovery's savings:

- put() now writes to a temp file and atomically renames it into place,
  instead of writing directly to the final path. The final path only
  ever exists fully written, so load() no longer needs a separate
  full-file read-and-checksum pass to guard against a corruption class
  the rename already rules out -- measured on M4, that redundant read
  was roughly half of load()'s total cost for a large (~2GB) entry.

- Optional KV quantization before writing (KVCache.to_quantized,
  --prompt-cache-disk-quantize-bits, unset by default): shrinks an
  entry's file size (and so its read/deserialize cost) by roughly the
  bit-width ratio versus fp16. A quantized entry promoted back into RAM
  stays quantized for the rest of that conversation (QuantizedKVCache is
  a full generation-time cache, not a disk-only format) -- a real
  output-quality tradeoff to measure before enabling by default, which
  is why it stays opt-in pending that measurement, not because the
  write/read path has any issue with it.

n_bytes accounting now reflects what was actually written when
quantization changes it, but still defers to the caller's own
entry.nbytes otherwise (existing tests construct entries with
deliberately synthetic declared sizes). 4 new tests (temp-file cleanup,
quantized round-trip and size reduction, quantize never mutates the
caller's own cache object); all 16 pass.
